## How It Works

Bell-LaPadula's **star property** (*-property) states that a subject cannot write to an object at a lower classification level than the subject's clearance. The purpose is to prevent information from flowing downward, protecting confidentiality. However, real systems require controlled downgrades when a document is properly sanitized. 

The model addresses this with the concept of a **trusted subject**. A trusted subject is not constrained by the *-property - it may write down, but only in accordance with explicit policy. The key distinction is that the trusted subject is part of the Trusted Computing Base (TCB) and performs only authorized sanitization functions. 

Without trusted subject designation, any process at TOP SECRET could write arbitrary TOP SECRET data to a SECRET queue. Even with sanitization logic in the application layer, if that application is not a trusted subject and is still bound by BLP, the write would be blocked. More critically, if the system incorrectly allows the application to bypass the property without being designated trusted, it creates an uncontrolled downgrade path. 

Some high assurance systems also consider **covert channels** - timing or storage channels that may leak information even when overt flows are blocked. The reference monitor must mediate all object accesses and some implementations add constraints to reduce covert bandwidth, though complete elimination is often impractical.

## Code Example

```javascript
class SecurityLabel {
  constructor(level, categorySet) {
    this.level = level; // 0=UNCLASS,1=CONF,2=SECRET,3=TOPSECRET
    this.categorySet = categorySet;
  }

  dominates(other) {
    // Simple domination for levels only
    return this.level >= other.level;
  }
}

class ReferenceMonitor {
  constructor() {
    this.trustedSubjects = new Set(['sanitizer-service']);
  }

  checkWrite(subject, object, isTrusted) {
    // Bell-LaPadula star property
    if (subject.clearance.dominates(object.label)) {
      return { allowed: true, reason: 'write to equal or higher level' };
    }

    // Write down attempt
    if (!object.label.dominates(subject.clearance)) {
      // Only trusted subjects may write down
      if (isTrusted && this.trustedSubjects.has(subject.name)) {
        // Must be audited - this is a controlled downgrade
        this.audit(subject, object, 'DOWNGRADE_WRITE');
        return { allowed: true, reason: 'trusted subject downgrade' };
      }
      return { allowed: false, reason: 'BLP *-property violation' };
    }

    return { allowed: false, reason: 'invalid' };
  }

  audit(subject, object, action) {
    console.log(`[AUDIT] ${action}: ${subject.name}(${subject.clearance.level}) -> ${object.name}(${object.label.level})`);
  }
}

// Usage
const rm = new ReferenceMonitor();
const ts = { name: 'app-service', clearance: new SecurityLabel(3, []) };
const queue = { name: 'secret-queue', label: new SecurityLabel(2, []) };

// Regular service cannot write down
console.log(rm.checkWrite(ts, queue, false));
// { allowed: false, reason: 'BLP *-property violation' }

// Trusted sanitizer can write down with audit
const sanitizer = { name: 'sanitizer-service', clearance: new SecurityLabel(3, []) };
console.log(rm.checkWrite(sanitizer, queue, true));
// { allowed: true, reason: 'trusted subject downgrade' }
```

## Key Points

- **Trust is explicit**: The *-property is absolute for normal subjects. Only subjects designated as trusted in the TCB policy may write down.
- **Downgrade is not automatic**: Sanitization in application code does not make an application a trusted subject - the system must designate it as trusted and constrain its behavior.
- **Audit is mandatory**: All controlled downgrades must be logged because they are inherently privileged operations that can leak data.
- **Covert channels remain a concern**: Even with perfect overt flow control, timing channels can leak information. High assurance systems use the reference monitor and may add noise or strict scheduling.

## Common Mistake

The most common error is conflating **application-layer sanitization** with **system-level trusted subject status**. Developers often assume that if they strip sensitive fields in code before writing to a lower-level queue, BLP is satisfied. But if the process is not designated as a trusted subject in the security policy, the reference monitor will block the write regardless of what the code does. Conversely, designating a process as trusted without rigorous verification creates massive risk - a bug in that trusted process can leak everything.

## Interview Tip

Interviewers are testing whether you understand the distinction between normal subjects and trusted subjects in BLP. A strong answer must state that write-down requires trusted subject designation, explain why application sanitization alone isn't sufficient without TCB designation, and mention that controlled downgrades must be audited. The best answers also note that even trusted subjects don't eliminate all risks (covert channels) and that the reference monitor enforces this centrally.
